==Post-match==
[[File:Steven Gerrard 2006.jpg|thumb|upright=0.75|The final is remembered for the contribution of Liverpool captain [[Steven Gerrard]]]]
Liverpool's victory meant they won the FA Cup for the seventh time, the club's manager [[Rafael Benítez]] stated that neither side deserved to lose the match: "It was difficult to say we deserved to win or the other team. It was a magnificent final. We knew that they could score goals but that we could also. We needed to keep going but after 63 games it was really difficult." Liverpool captain [[Steven Gerrard]] also praised West Ham, stating: "West Ham were brilliant, they gave us a really good game but we had a never-say-die attitude and we stuck in there, our best chance was on penalties as we did not have any energy left."<ref>{{cite news |title=Benitez grateful to win thriller |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/teams/l/liverpool/4764633.stm |work=BBC Sport|date=13 May 2006 |access-date=17 May 2015 }}</ref> Due to the nature of the comeback, the match has come to be regarded as ''The Gerrard Final'',<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.belfasttelegraph.co.uk/sport/football/from-stanley-matthews-to-steven-gerrard-five-classic-fa-cup-finals-39046820.html |title=From Stanley Matthews to Steven Gerrard, five classic FA Cup final |work=Belfast Telegraph|date=15 March 2020 |access-date=26 April 2020 |first=Andy |last=Hampson }}</ref> teammate [[Peter Crouch]] summed up the effect Gerrard had on the match: "When the injury-time board went up we thought our chance had gone, but it is always good to have a player like Steven Gerrard in your team."<ref>{{cite web |title=When Steven Gerrard took the 2006 FA Cup Final by storm |url=http://www.thefa.com/news/the-fa-cup/2014/may/steven-gerrard-illustration-match-programme |website=TheFA.com |publisher=The Football Association |date=2 January 2015 |access-date=17 May 2015 }}</ref> A [[BBC Sport]] poll in May 2015 deemed Gerrard's goal to be the best in an FA Cup Final from the past 50 years.<ref>{{cite news|title=FA Cup final: The greatest goal from the last 50 years voted by you|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/32628391|access-date=4 August 2015|work=BBC Sport|date=27 May 2015}}</ref>

West Ham manager [[Alan Pardew]] described the defeat as hard to take: "It felt like a defeat when the third goal went in. We rallied and had a great chance in extra time. We had to dig in. This was a top team we were playing, but I felt we were going to win. Only a 35-yard Gerrard smasher was going to change that. The players have been magnificent – we are proud of the way we played." Captain [[Nigel Reo-Coker]] found defeat hard to take as he felt they had done enough to win the final: "I can't describe it how I feel at the moment – gutted because I think we deserved to win this game. The players put in a fantastic effort, but it just wasn't to be for us. It was a marvellous FA Cup final and we gave all we could. I hope everyone was proud – it just wasn't our day."<ref>{{cite news|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/teams/w/west_ham_utd/4764715.stm |title=Cup loss hard to take for Pardew |work=BBC Sport|date=13 May 2006 |access-date=17 May 2015 }}</ref>

Liverpool's victory set up a [[FA Community Shield|Community Shield]] match against [[Chelsea F.C.|Chelsea]], winners of the [[2005–06 Premier League]]. As FA Cup winners, Liverpool would have been awarded qualification into the [[UEFA Europa League|UEFA Cup]], but because they had qualified for the [[UEFA Champions League]] via their league position, the UEFA Cup place was passed to runners-up, West Ham.<ref name="mom"/>

